## Why This Is Asked
Tests around feature flags in E2E flows are common in production environments. This evaluates the ability to design deterministic tests for both flag states, validate calculations, and ensure order completion under different configurations.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags and gated flows
- API-driven data seeding for test setup
- Network and service mocking for external dependencies such as tax and fraud services
- Deterministic assertions and test isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test skeleton
async function testFlag(flagValue) {
  // Toggle feature flag via API
  await api.post('/api/feature-flags', { flag: 'new-checkout', enabled: flagValue });

  // Seed cart and user
  await api.post('/api/test/seed', { userId: 'user-123', items: [...] });

  // Mock external services
  await page.route('**/api/tax*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { rate: flagValue ? 0.085 : 0 } }));
  await page.route('**/api/fraud*', route => route.fulfill({ body: { status: 'pass' } }));

  // Navigate to checkout and complete flow
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await page.click('#place-order');

  // Assert final total and order completion
  const finalTotal = await page.textContent('#total');
  expect(finalTotal).toBe(flagValue ? '$108.50' : '$100.00');
  expect(await page.locator('.order-success')).toBeVisible();
}

// Run tests for both flag states
test('Legacy checkout', () => testFlag(false));
test('New checkout', () => testFlag(true));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle rate limiting on the feature flag API?
- What strategies would you use to debug flaky tests in the fraud check flow?
- How do you ensure test data isolation between parallel test runs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests involving asynchronous exports and email notifications are common in SaaS applications. This question evaluates practical familiarity with end-to-end testing flows, mocking external services, and ensuring idempotency under rapid repeated actions.

## Key Concepts

- Asynchronous job orchestration and eventual consistency
- Mocking external services (export service and email delivery)
- Test data isolation and seeding
- Idempotency and race condition handling

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('data export flow is idempotent', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/dashboard');

  await seedTestData({ userId: 'user-123', dataset: [...] });

  await page.fill('#date-from', '2024-01-01');
  await page.fill('#date-to', '2024-01-31');
  await page.selectOption('#status', 'completed');

  await page.click('#export-btn');

  await page.route('**/api/export', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: { jobId: 'job-123', status: 'completed' } });
  });

  await page.route('**/api/email/send', async (route) => {
    await route.fulfill({ status: 200 });
  });

  await expect(page.locator('#download-link')).toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 });

  const emailRequests = [];
  await page.on('request', (req) => {
    if (req.url().includes('/api/email/send')) {
      emailRequests.push(req);
    }
  });

  await page.click('#export-btn');
  await page.waitForTimeout(500);

  expect(emailRequests).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

