## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical E2E testing with per-user feature flags, data isolation, and telemetry verification in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags per-user, test data isolation, mocking services
- End-to-end UI verification and telemetry validation
- Flakiness prevention in a shared staging environment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example (high level)
const flagApi = new MockFlagService({ userA: true, userB: false });

test('enabled path renders tour', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs('userA');
  await flagApi.setUserFlag('userA', true);
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  await expect(page.locator('.tour-step')).toBeVisible();
  // complete tour steps
});

test('disabled path skips tour', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs('userB');
  await flagApi.setUserFlag('userB', false);
  await page.goto('/onboarding');
  await expect(page.locator('.tour-step')).toHaveCount(0);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for multiple flags and regions?
- What strategies ensure telemetry data remains accurate across retries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first PWAs are a common beginner challenge, ensuring service workers and syncing work under real user conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service worker caching
- E2E data seeding for deterministic tests
- Network control via intercepts (cy.intercept / route)
- Idempotent sync and duplicate prevention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress example sketch
cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', {user:'test', products:[101,102]});
cy.visit('/');
cy.intercept('/api/cart', { statusCode: 503 }).as('cartOffline');
cy.get('#add-101').click();
cy.get('#add-102').click();
cy.intercept('/api/cart', (req) => req.continue()).as('cartOnline');
cy.wait('@cartOffline');
cy.get('#checkout').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a multi-user scenario with concurrent offline sessions?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the offline sync is fast enough for a good UX?

